liation in a year and it can result  up to a 50 percent total yield reduction  it is also macrocyclic in hetero asia so  this means that we'll go through all  five of the spore stages that we heard  about before  and requires an alternate host to  complete them so the alternate host for  the figure I have here would be the  balsam first so it's more of a tree rest  like I said it's got that potential for  sexual recombination each year so you  can imagine when you've got willow  that's in the field for 20-plus years  that may have been resistant to rest  when you planted it you have something  that's sexually recombining every year  so that resistance is probably not gonna  be there after a few years of sexual  recombination and it is described by  mainly by these yellow to orange holes  that are on the underside of the leaf  and this is how we used to phenotype  severity based on the percent coverage  of a leaf by using these sports so  because it is so complicated there's a  lot of diversity within Melara but like  I said there's a lot of diversity within  Salix we wanted to try and kind of use  that diversity in Salix to identify  resistance genes that can be sort of  pyramid in to make sure plantings in  that SRC system so typically they're  grown in monoculture but if you the best  form of management the most effective is  to make sure plant with different levels  of resistance so obviously we need to  know about those resistances before we  can advise what kind of mixtures need to  be planted and so two different mapping  populations were constructed to get more  information on the shrub willow so the  first one is a Salix purpurea f2  population that kind of takes center  stage and other aspects of my research  but for the time being I'm going to talk  about the other eight families that are  surrounding it so these eight families  comprise the common parent what we call  for short just the common parent mapping  population so we utilize the naturalized  Salix purpurea which is the nine four  six and nine four oh one is these purple  boxes so four families share it's my  instinct to move in my hand but I'm  gonna do this  so four of these families share nine  four oh six as the common female parent  and the other four will use nine four oh  one as the common male parent we say  like such a wince s in women Alice  having a reciprocal cross with Enid  Salix women Alice is important because  it's the most widely grown species of  Salix in biomass in Europe and so a lot  of the studies in this system have been  conducted using Salix women Alice on  European species of Malaya so all of  these individuals have been sequenced  using GPS and so I put together this PCA  of those eight families so you can kind  of see the common parents line four one  nine four six or kind of splitting the  families where I'm doing it again see  none 401 and nine 406 are kind of  splitting the two families up where when  we look at PC one we can see that these  two families here are the Salix of women  Alice that I just mentioned where this  one is sale excu densest so PC one is  really separating these families  phylogenetically so these three are in  the section femen where the families on  the other side of the PCA are from the  section helix so they're more related  where PC two is doing the work of  separating out each family so you can  see we have this family here with the f1  kind of lying somewhere in the middle as  we would expect it to do so now that we  have our GPS data if I want to map GTL I  need to construct maps for everything so  I put together this general pipeline of  the way that I handled that GPS data so  I use tassel GPS to call variants which  depending on the family resulted between  three and five hundred thousand markers  in each family these were then passed  into the tassel GUI and I filtered for  up to 80% presence which really cut down  those number of markers they were then  subset in our four back cross markers so  we then instead of dealing with one  population now we're dealing with two  separate maps for each one for each  parent based on the parental genotype  and then I used a combination of our  QTL and a s map to do some additional  filtering and for determining linkage  groups and establishing the marker order  and so each map at the end of this  process has between one and three  thousand markers so we have 16 maps in  total two for each of the families so  again now that we've got the maps this  makes it sound so easy now they've got  the maps we need the phenotype data to  really kind of bring it together  unfortunately leaf rust has not been  very cooperative with me in this study  so in the years 2016 and 2018 we had  negligible to no leaf rust presence in  the field at all  so we're really hoping 2019 we can  actually get a good survey but the one  that we were able to do 2017 in reality  is a mild rest year but we can still  there's still things to learn from this  and I should mention that all of these  families are planted up at Cornell  Agrotech in Geneva there's four  replications it's about four thousand  plots for all families together and so  they've all been surveyed so now that  I've had this plot up for a little while  let me try and explain it a little bit  so the two purple bars are the common  parents so there's nine for a six here a  nine 4:01 the vertical line in the  middle delineates the families that are  using that common female or common male  parents so as you look at each f1 which  are these larger boxes the female is  always on the left of the f1 with the  male next to it so get the comment the  female male f f1 male f1 male and it  switches on the other side each dot  represents a genotypic mean to kind of  give you an idea even though the mean is  here we still have a wide range of leaf  rust severity through these populations  so when I finally got this figure worked  out one of the first things that jumped  out to me is how each family kind of has  its own story with leaf rest so in the  case of this family here  the 4:07 family we can see that the mean  of the f1 is kind of between the parents  which is what we think more of as an  additive model with the between the two  parents but if we look at 4:43 on the  end there seems to be a breakdown in  resistance and that's common throughout  some of the other families as well so  even though like I said even though this  was a mild year  we're still getting story about leaf  rust with each of these separate  families so it's important to kind of  think of them as a population but also  as an individual families as we kind of  think about the results that we find so  now that I've got the maps constructed  and a phenotype data I actually was able  to identify a few QTL and so I just  pulled one out so I could show you so  this is a map from one of the such a  winces families it's a map of the female  common parent that I put together and we  were able to find a QTL on chromosome 7  so you can see this kind of  National Monument shaped GTL here has  this nice peak here which actually maybe  a little bit lucky is a marker it's an  actual marker and so pulling that out  looking at the allele effects you can  see that the anything homozygous at that  marker there is still a rating here but  a lot of them are at zero and it's very  mild whereas anything heterozygous at  that low sigh has a higher higher  phenotype suggesting maybe partial it's  conferring may be partial resistance at  that so going forward with these maps I  really want to as I said a few times  each family kind of has its own  personality and so I want to go through  each of the genetic maps and kind of  address some of the eccentricities there  before I finalized them and content with  with what their with their status once  that's done I'll be able to go through  and finalize the list of leaf rescue TL  but I was able to find and naturally  I've been applying I've been going  through those fields or a lot of  separate traits and so like I want to  use those traits as well and I would  also like to try a way to analyze all  eight families together I'm kind of  bouncing around with that but I want to  make sure I get these maps done before I  move on so with that in mind I'd like to  thank everyone for coming and I also  like to thank everyone at the Larry  smart lab with a special shout out to  Rebecca  Lauren Jane and Dawn who are our  technicians  it's pretty much everyone here knows the  lab would fall apart without them so  we're just really grateful for them and  also Holly and chase from the Chris  smart lab so I like to thank everyone  again and I can take questions  [Applause]  any here's what you don't have roster  this actually don't have bus could you  somehow visually a not so the question  was and use that we don't have rest can  we artificially inoculate some of the  right yeah so I know that that has been  done so I mentioned at row well he'll  he's kind of my partner in crime and  Plant Pathology and Chris smart slab so  he has done some work with different  isolates of rest in these different  families but as far as well my work is  done it's really important that we can  get filled so the question was are we  after complete resistance or with some  partial resistance be enough and  honestly as I said when you're dealing  with a mixture planting it's really best  to have different levels of resistance  because obviously you don't want to  encourage kind of that selection you  kind of want to let it you almost want  to let it have something so that it's  there's a lot of selection pressure so  in terms of if we care about absolute or  partial I think I'm open to whatever I  can find it's really about kind of  characterizing what we have so the  question is what if we know about the  race structure with in Milan's Rafi so  there is a lot of work on that I know  Chris smarts lab has done some work on  that but definitely compared 